//- Install and run. Params: d.
div(data-ui="prose")
  p Fablecut runs on your own computer. The studio is a small web server that you open in your browser; nothing is uploaded anywhere, and no account is needed.

  h2#requirements What you need
  div(role="region" aria-label="Requirements" tabindex="0")
    table
      thead
        tr
          th Software
          th Why
          th How to check
      tbody
        tr
          td
            strong Node.js 24 or newer
          td Runs the studio, the renderer and the MCP server.
          td
            raw md("`node --version` prints v24 or higher")
        tr
          td
            strong FFmpeg
          td Encodes the frames into MP4 and mixes the sound.
          td
            raw md("`ffmpeg -version` prints a version")
        tr
          td
            strong A modern browser
          td Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari, for the studio's screens.
          td Open any page of the studio
        tr
          td Claude Code (optional)
          td Lets Claude build clips for you and answer your requests.
          td
            raw md("`claude --version`")
  raw p("FFmpeg is found on your `PATH`. If it lives somewhere else, set `FFMPEG_PATH` to the program's full path. On Windows an FFmpeg installed with **winget** is found automatically.")
  +callout(variant="info")
    @content
      raw p("**Voice and transcription are optional.** The studio can speak a script and time every word with free local tools (Kokoro and whisper.cpp). You can skip them until you want a narrated video: see [Set up voice and transcription](/docs/sound#voice-setup).")

  h2#start Start the studio
  p Open a terminal in the folder where you have Fablecut (the repository), then run:
  pre
    code
      | npm install
      | npm run studio
  raw p("The first command installs what the studio needs (once). The second starts the studio and prints its address, `http://127.0.0.1:8787`. Open that address in your browser. Leave the terminal open: closing it, or pressing [[Ctrl+C]] in it, stops the studio.")
  raw p("To change the port or the address, set `PORT` and `HOST` before starting, for example `PORT=8800 npm run studio`. The studio answers only to `localhost`, `127.0.0.1` and `::1` unless you bind another address on purpose.")

  h2#showcase Fill the library with the showcase (optional)
  p A fresh studio has an empty library apart from its fonts. The repository holds the recipe of eleven showcase clips that build a large library of reusable assets. To rebuild them:
  div(role="region" aria-label="Showcase commands" tabindex="0")
    table
      thead
        tr
          th Command
          th What it does
      tbody
        tr
          td
            raw md("`npm run seed`")
          td Builds the library and the showcase clips without rendering any video. Takes a few minutes. Start here.
        tr
          td
            raw md("`npm run showcase`")
          td The same, and renders every showcase clip to MP4. Leave a few gigabytes of disk free: some renders are 180 to 600 MB.
        tr
          td
            raw md("`npm run sync-assets`")
          td
            raw md("Pushes every asset in the `assets/` folder into the library (only new or changed ones). See [Keep assets/ and the library in step](/docs/repo#sync-assets).")

  h2#where-work-goes Where your work is kept
  raw p("Everything you make lives in one folder, the **data directory**: `./data` by default (this studio uses `" + d.live.dataDir + "`). It holds the database (`studio.db`), thumbnails, uploaded files, baked sequences, cached audio and your renders. Git ignores it.")
  ul
    li
      raw md("To keep two separate libraries, start the studio with another data directory: `STUDIO_DATA=/path/to/other npm run studio`.")
    li
      raw md("To back up your work, stop the studio and copy the data directory.")
    li
      raw md("Exports and evidence from the command-line scripts go to `./output`, also ignored by git.")
  raw p("Every setting the studio reads is listed in [Settings and folders](/docs/config).")

  h2#claude-setup Connect Claude Code (optional)
  raw p("The repository registers the studio's MCP server in its `.mcp.json`, so **Claude Code started in this folder gets the studio's tools automatically**: run `claude` in the repository folder and approve the `studio` server when it asks. Claude then works on the same data directory as the studio in your browser, and what it makes appears on your screen at once.")
  raw p("Read [Claude Code and the studio](/docs/claude) next if you want Claude to build videos for you.")

  h2#check-it-works Check that it works
  ol
    li
      raw md("Open `http://127.0.0.1:8787`. You see **Library** with a search box. An empty library says so; that is fine.")
    li
      raw md("Open **Clips** and press **New clip**. Give it a name such as `hello`, pick a format and press **Create clip**. The clip editor opens.")
    li
      raw md("Press **Add item** and pick any visual asset (a clip with nothing on its visual tracks cannot be rendered).")
    li
      raw md("Press **Render**, keep **Normal**, and press **Render**. The **Renders** screen shows the progress, and the finished video appears in the **Gallery**.")
  raw p("If a step fails, [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting) lists the usual causes: a missing FFmpeg, a port in use, a stopped server.")

  h2#run-the-tests For developers: the checks
  pre
    code
      | npm test              # the whole test suite
      | npm run typecheck     # TypeScript over the JavaScript sources
      | npm run lint          # ESLint
      | npm run verify        # version pinning and determinism, checked by frame hash
  raw p("More commands are in [Commands and scripts](/docs/scripts).")
